# Command-line interface

@lede
Three binaries: `brief` (the compiler), `brief-web` (the static-site
generator), and `brief-lsp` (the language server). Everything else
lives behind a subcommand.
@end

## `brief`

The compiler. Subcommands: `compile`, `convert`, `explain`, `fmt`,
`watch`.

### `brief compile`

Compile a `.brf` file to one of three targets.

```bash
brief compile <file.brf> --target=<html|llm|json> [options]
```

Options:

@dl
--target=html | llm | json
: The output format. Default is `html`, override in `brief.toml` with
  `compile.default_target`.
-w, --write
: Write the output to a file next to the source. `.brf` becomes `.html`,
  `.txt`, or `.json` depending on the target. The compiler refuses to
  write when the derived path equals the input (e.g. compiling a `.txt`
  with `--target=llm`), so a source file can never be overwritten.
-o, --output PATH
: Write to a specific path. Mutually exclusive with `-w`.
--report-tokens
: Only valid with `--target=llm`. Prints a stderr line with a rough
  token estimate of the output (characters divided by four).
--strip-emphasis
: LLM target only. Drop emphasis markers entirely; text content survives.
--keep-asset-urls
: LLM target only. Preserve image URLs in `@image` rendering. Default is
  to drop them (the alt text alone reaches the LLM).
--keep-table-rule
: LLM target only. Emit a `|---|` line under table headers. Default
  omits it to save tokens.
--keep-metadata
: LLM target only. Emit frontmatter as-is rather than stripping it.
@end

Exit codes:

- `0` — success.
- `1` — one or more compile errors. Diagnostics go to `stderr`.

### `brief convert`

Convert between Markdown and Brief. Direction comes from the input
extension: `.md` converts to Brief, `.brf` exports to Markdown.

```bash
brief convert notes.md                      # writes notes.brf
brief convert notes.md -o renamed.brf       # rename single output
brief convert notes.md --stdout             # pipe to stdout
brief convert *.md                          # batch
brief convert notes.brf                     # reverse: writes notes.md
brief convert docs/*.brf                    # batch export to Markdown
```

Both directions are *lossy by design*. Constructs without a clean
equivalent on the other side are flagged as design holes and written to
stderr. Converting to Brief also inserts `// TODO[B-hole:...]` comments
at the source location; inline HTML, raw HTML blocks, and YAML
frontmatter are the most common holes. Exporting to Markdown flags
underline (emitted as `<u>`), comments (dropped), custom shortcodes
(expanded via their `template_html`), and code-fence attributes
(dropped).

Converted output checks itself before it is written: Brief output must
compile, and Markdown output must survive a round-trip back through the
Markdown→Brief converter. `--no-strict` opts out. A `.brf` input that
does not compile fails the conversion — fix the source first.

Per-file failures in batch mode do not abort the run; each file is
converted independently.

### `brief explain`

Print the long-form explanation of an error code.

```bash
brief explain B0204
```

Output is the rule, why it exists, a worked example of the error, and a
worked example of the fix. Every code the compiler can emit has an entry.
See @ref[reference/error-codes.brf](Error code registry) for the full
list.

### `brief fmt`

Reformat Brief source into the canonical style — gofmt doctrine, no
configuration beyond one explicit opt-in.

```bash
brief fmt doc.brf              # formatted source to stdout
brief fmt doc.brf --write      # rewrite in place
brief fmt src/*.brf --check    # exit non-zero if anything would change
brief fmt doc.brf --sort-frontmatter --write
```

### `brief watch`

Watch files or directories and recompile on change, with a 100ms
debounce.

```bash
brief watch src/ --target=html
brief watch doc.brf --target=llm --no-clear
```

Accepts the same target and LLM-mode flags as `brief compile`, plus
`--no-clear` to keep previous output on screen between runs.

## `brief-lsp`

The language server. No subcommands, no flags — editors spawn it over
stdio.

It runs the same pipeline as `brief compile`, project context included:
`brief.toml` is discovered by walking up from each open file, custom
shortcodes and `compile.strict_heading_levels` apply, and `@ref` targets
are validated against a project-wide index. Unsaved buffers count —
rename a heading anchor and every open document referencing it updates
immediately, without saving.

@dl
Diagnostics
: The full compile diagnostic set on open, change, and save. A broken
  `brief.toml` is reported as an error pinned to the top of the file.
Hover
: The `brief explain` text for the diagnostic under the cursor.
Goto definition
: On a `@ref`, jump to the target heading (or the top of the target
  file for whole-file references).
Find references
: On a heading anchor or a `@ref`, list every reference to that target
  across the project.
Completion
: Shortcode names after `@`, project file paths inside `@ref[`, and
  heading anchors after `#`.
Document symbols
: The heading outline, nested by level.
Formatting
: Whole-document `brief fmt`.
@end

## `brief-web`

The static-site generator. Subcommands: `init`, `build`, `serve`.

### `brief-web init`

Scaffold a new project.

```bash
brief-web init my-site            # single-page mode (just index.brf)
brief-web init my-site --multi    # multi-page mode (with SUMMARY.brf)
brief-web init my-site --force    # overwrite existing files
```

### `brief-web build`

One-shot build. Reads `book.toml`, walks `src/`, writes `dist/`.

```bash
brief-web build                # build the project in the current directory
brief-web build path/to/site   # build a specific project
```

The previous `dist/` contents are cleared before each build.

### `brief-web serve`

Build, then run a development HTTP server with file watching.

```bash
brief-web serve                              # default: 127.0.0.1:3000
brief-web serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080
brief-web serve --no-watch                   # one-shot serve, no live reload
```

A tiny client is injected into every served page. It listens to a
Server-Sent-Events stream at `/__brief_web_events` and reloads the page
when the build finishes. No WebSockets, no polling.

## Common patterns

```bash
# Render and budget a prompt.
brief compile prompt.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > prompt.txt

# Round-trip a Markdown document.
brief convert notes.md && brief compile notes.brf --target=html -w

# Live-edit a docs site.
brief-web serve docs/ &
$EDITOR docs/src/some-page.brf
```
